      <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "http://schema.org", "@type": "MedicalWebPage", "about": {"@type": "WebPage", "alternateName": ["Tooth knocked out"], "name": "Knocked-out tooth"}, "author": {"@type": "Organization", "email": "nhswebsite.servicedesk@nhs.net", "logo": "https://assets.nhs.uk/nhsuk-cms/images/nhs-attribution.width-510.png", "name": "NHS website", "url": "https://www.nhs.uk"}, "breadcrumb": {"@context": "http://schema.org", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "item": {"@id": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/", "genre": [], "name": "Conditions A to Z"}, "position": 0}, {"@type": "ListItem", "item": {"@id": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/knocked-out-tooth/", "genre": ["Accident or injury"], "name": "Knocked-out tooth"}, "position": 1}]}, "copyrightHolder": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Crown Copyright"}, "dateModified": "2025-04-09T12:34:26+00:00", "description": "Find out more about how to save a knocked-out tooth before seeing a dentist.", "genre": ["Accident or injury"], "hasPart": [{"@type": "HealthTopicContent", "description": "If you've knocked your tooth out, you should not touch the root. Clean it if it's dirty and try to put it back in place, or put it in milk.", "hasHealthAspect": "http://schema.org/SelfCareHealthAspect", "hasPart": [{"@type": "WebPageElement", "headline": "", "text": "<p>There are things you can do yourself before seeing a dentist if your tooth has been knocked out.</p><ol><li>Hold the tooth by the white bit that sticks out of the gum (the crown) \u2013 do not touch the root.</li><li>If the tooth is dirty, rinse it gently in milk, saline or saliva.</li><li>Try to put it back into the hole in the gum.</li><li>Bite down gently on a clean cloth to hold the tooth in place if it goes back in easily.</li><li>Go to a dentist as soon as possible.</li></ol>"}, {"@type": "WebPageElement", "headline": "Important", "text": "<p>Do not put a baby tooth back in as it could damage the tooth growing underneath.</p>"}, {"@type": "WebPageElement", "headline": "", "text": "<p>If you cannot put the tooth back in, you can put it in either milk or saliva.</p><p>Spit into a container (if it's your tooth) or get your child to spit into a container (if it's theirs) if you're putting the tooth in saliva.</p>"}], "headline": "What to do if a tooth has been knocked out", "url": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/knocked-out-tooth/#self-care"}, {"@type": "HealthTopicContent", "description": "", "hasHealthAspect": "http://schema.org/MedicalHelpEmergencyHealthAspect", "hasPart": [{"@type": "WebPageElement", "headline": "Ask for an emergency dentist appointment if:", "identifier": "immediate", "text": "<ul>\n<li>\n  you have knocked out a tooth, even if you cannot find it\n </li>\n</ul>\n"}, {"@type": "WebPageElement", "headline": "Information", "text": "<h3>To see a dentist out of hours:</h3><ul><li>call your dentist \u2013 if they're closed, their answerphone may tell you what to do</li></ul><h3>If you do not have a dentist or cannot get an emergency appointment:</h3><ul><li>call 111 \u2013 they can advise you what to do</li><li><a href=\"https://www.nhs.uk/service-search/find-a-dentist/\">find a dentist near you</a> \u2013 ask if you can have an emergency appointment</li></ul><p>You may have to pay for your appointment.</p><p>Read more about <a href=\"/nhs-services/dentists/how-much-nhs-dental-treatment-costs/\">how much NHS dental treatment costs</a>.</p>"}], "headline": "", "url": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/knocked-out-tooth/#emergency-medical-help-old"}, {"@type": "HealthTopicContent", "description": "A dentist will check a knocked-out tooth is in the right place, fix a loose tooth in position, or replace it with a denture (false tooth) if needed.", "hasHealthAspect": "http://schema.org/TreatmentsHealthAspect", "hasPart": [{"@type": "WebPageElement", "headline": "", "text": "<p>If you put the tooth back in, the dentist will check it's in the right place and move it if they need to.</p><p>You may need an X-ray to check if the root of the tooth has been damaged.</p><p>If you brought the tooth in milk or saliva, the dentist will usually:</p><ul><li>clean the tooth and put it back in</li><li>fix the tooth to the teeth on either side of it to hold it in place (splinting)</li></ul><p>You'll probably need to go back after a couple of weeks for other treatment, such as a root canal treatment, and to have the splint removed.</p><p>If you cannot find the tooth or the dentist is not able to save it, the tooth may be replaced with a <a href=\"/tests-and-treatments/dentures/\">denture (false tooth)</a> while the socket heals.</p><p>Once it's healed, there may be other options like a bridge or dental implant.</p><p>You may have to pay for your appointment and treatment.</p>"}, {"@type": "WebPageElement", "headline": "Cost of dental treatment", "text": "<div class=\"block-richtext\">\n<p>NHS dental treatment is free for some people, including:</p><ul><li>children under 18 years old, or under 19 and in full-time education</li><li>if you're pregnant or have had a baby in the past 12 months</li><li>if you're on some benefits, including Income Support or Universal Credit</li></ul><p>If you do not qualify for free dental care, you will usually have to pay for treatment.</p><p><a href=\"/nhs-services/dentists/how-much-nhs-dental-treatment-costs/\">Find out more about NHS dental costs</a></p>\n</div>"}], "headline": "Treatment for a knocked-out tooth", "url": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/knocked-out-tooth/#medical-treatments"}, {"@type": "HealthTopicContent", "description": "A knocked-out tooth can usually be saved by putting it back in place, or in milk, as soon as possible before seeing a dentist.", "hasHealthAspect": "http://schema.org/OverviewHealthAspect", "hasPart": [{"@type": "WebPageElement", "headline": "Knocked-out tooth", "text": "Self-care"}, {"@type": "WebPageElement", "headline": "Knocked-out tooth", "text": "Medical treatments"}, {"@type": "WebPageElement", "text": "<p>If you've knocked your tooth out, you should not touch the root. Clean it if it's dirty and try to put it back in place, or put it in milk.</p> "}, {"@type": "WebPageElement", "text": "<p>A dentist will check a knocked-out tooth is in the right place, fix a loose tooth in position, or replace it with a denture (false tooth) if needed.</p> "}], "headline": "", "url": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/knocked-out-tooth/#overview"}], "keywords": "", "lastReviewed": ["2025-02-06T08:58:00+00:00", "2028-02-06T08:58:00+00:00"], "license": "https://developer.api.nhs.uk/terms", "name": "Knocked-out tooth", "relatedLink": [{"@type": "LinkRole", "linkRelationship": "Navigation", "name": "Knocked-out tooth", "position": 0, "url": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/knocked-out-tooth/"}], "schemaVersion": "http://schema.org/version/13.0/", "url": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/knocked-out-tooth/"}</script>
    

    
      
      
    

